  "worked_solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/1cd695e6f1fab35a692fde9003d452862db33a894c23657eb87f8d773de8cbb9.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/5a9871ac900e85c9f47118f6233b2e7f094af010dcb73e54221cadac01ce1644.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=2({3}^{x})-4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930907/assets/89a7ee023e8d4232b617052efdc9844cb00b2e3f05968119cea11c89242ca61a.png)",
